      <p>Taiwan's response, as MODA described it through Focus Taiwan: the National Institute of Cyber Security issued warning alerts beginning July 20, 2026. MODA stated that "the relevant attack sources, methods, and scope of impact have all been fully investigated, and the affected units have successively completed their handling."</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="signal">
      <h2><span class="num">05 — Signal</span>The Surface That Isn't There</h2>
      <p>The enforcement surface that stopped the GTG-1002 campaign was a property of Anthropic's business model. Account-based access generated telemetry. Telemetry enabled detection. Detection enabled account bans. Bans stopped the campaign. Every step in that chain depended on the attack running through a provider's commercial API.</p>
      <p>When the same class of capability runs on open-source agent harnesses — as it did in the Taiwan campaign and the knaithe campaign and five other incidents in Tenable's cluster — that chain does not exist. No provider holds an account. No server generates telemetry. No entity has the authority, the access, or the visibility to intervene. Discovery, in both the Taiwan and knaithe cases, came through accident: an inadvertently exposed archive and a misconfigured home directory. Neither campaign was caught by any defensive system during execution.</p>
      <p>The AISI finding is not theoretical. It describes the operational reality demonstrated by these campaigns: deployment-time safety measures cannot be applied to software that has already been released under an MIT license. The license is irrevocable. The tools are available to anyone. The safety bypass — framing an intrusion as "authorized penetration testing" — is a system-prompt-level technique that any operator can replicate.</p>
      <p>The cost asymmetry compounds the structural gap. AISI found that running an automated cyber task with DeepSeek V4-Pro costs $0.28 per task, compared to $12.50 for the same task on Anthropic's Opus 4.5 — a factor of 45. The open-source path is not just ungovernable. It is 45 times cheaper.</p>
      <p>Hermes and OpenClaw remain available under the same MIT license. No government has proposed a mechanism for applying post-release safety controls to open-source agent frameworks. The only reason this campaign is known is that someone made a mistake with an archive. The only reason the knaithe campaign is known is that Hermes misconfigured a directory. Tenable documented seven incidents across three actors — all within a span of months — using the same freely available tools. The question is not whether this will happen again. It is how many times it has already happened — with the archive still closed.</p>
